mortgage means a loan to buy a house.

6
00:00:19.245 --> 00:00:21.430
They'll pay off their mortgage in twenty years.

7
00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:24.202
Your turn. Say: mortgage.

8
00:00:26.902 --> 00:00:27.834
mortgage.

9
00:00:28.850 --> 00:00:29.642
pension.

10
00:00:30.342 --> 00:00:33.596
pension means money paid regularly to someone after they retire.

11
00:00:33.896 --> 00:00:36.081
A good pension makes retirement easier.

12
00:00:36.781 --> 00:00:38.597
Your turn. Say: pension.

13
00:00:41.297 --> 00:00:42.055
pension.

14
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:43.830
tax.

15
00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:46.844
tax means money people pay to the government.

16
00:00:47.144 --> 00:00:49.724
Income tax pays for schools and roads.

17
00:00:50.424 --> 00:00:52.786
Your turn. Say: tax.

18
00:00:55.486 --> 00:00:56.313
tax.

19
00:00:57.317 --> 00:00:58.306
stock market.

20
00:00:59.006 --> 00:01:02.469
stock market means where shares in companies are bought and sold.

21
00:01:02.769 --> 00:01:05.058
The stock market fell sharply last week.

22
00:01:05.758 --> 00:01:08.376
Your turn. Say: stock market.

23
00:01:11.076 --> 00:01:12.507
stock market.

24
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:14.622
cost-effective.

25
00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:18.343
cost-effective means giving good results for the money spent.

26
00:01:18.643 --> 00:01:21.165
Solar panels are a cost-effective investment.

27
00:01:21.865 --> 00:01:24.239
Your turn. Say: cost-effective.

28
00:01:26.939 --> 00:01:28.196
cost-effective.

29
00:01:29.183 --> 00:01:30.289
bankruptcy.

30
00:01:30.989 --> 00:01:34.591
bankruptcy means when a person or company cannot pay their debts.

31
00:01:34.891 --> 00:01:37.540
The shop went into bankruptcy after the pandemic.

32
00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:40.835
Your turn. Say: bankruptcy.

33
00:01:43.535 --> 00:01:44.710
bankruptcy.

34
00:01:45.717 --> 00:01:46.729
loan repayment.

35
00:01:47.429 --> 00:01:50.938
loan repayment means money you pay back to the bank each month.

36
00:01:51.238 --> 00:01:53.830
My loan repayment is two hundred dollars a month.

37
00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.985
Your turn. Say: loan repayment.

38
00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:00.976
loan repayment.

39
00:02:01.983 --> 00:02:03.379
financial literacy.

40
00:02:04.079 --> 00:02:07.240
financial literacy means understanding how to manage money.

41
00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:09.841
Financial literacy should be taught at school.

42
00:02:10.541 --> 00:02:13.530
Your turn. Say: financial literacy.

43
00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:17.649
financial literacy.

44
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.650
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.181
I wish I had learned about money earlier.

46
00:02:24.483 --> 00:02:27.240
Many people feel that. What would you do differently?

47
00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:32.805
If only I hadn't borrowed so much! My loan repayments are huge.

48
00:02:34.117 --> 00:02:36.151
Have you thought about saving for a pension?

49
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.112
Not yet. I wish I understood the stock market better.

50
00:02:42.417 --> 00:02:46.532
Start with financial literacy courses. They're cost-effective and very useful.

51
00:02:47.817 --> 00:02:50.850
Grammar focus: wish and if only.

52
00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:57.062
Use wish or if only with the past simple for present regrets, and with the past perfect for past regrets.

53
00:02:57.362 --> 00:02:59.721
Use wish plus would for things that annoy you.

54
00:03:00.021 --> 00:03:03.274
For example: If only I had saved when I was younger!

55
00:03:03.574 --> 00:03:06.329
If only is stronger and more emotional than I wish.

56
00:03:07.317 --> 00:03:10.422
Now read and listen. Lessons Nobody Taught Us.

57
00:03:10.722 --> 00:03:13.975
Many adults wish they had learned financial literacy at school.

58
00:03:15.283 --> 00:03:18.165
Without it, people often take loans they cannot afford.

59
00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.657
Some end up in bankruptcy because of high loan repayments.

60
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:28.422
Understanding tax, pensions and the stock market helps people plan for the future.

61
00:03:29.717 --> 00:03:33.876
As one expert said: if only schools taught money as well as maths!

62
00:03:35.183 --> 00:03:37.418
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

63
00:03:37.718 --> 00:03:39.543
A loan to buy a house is a blank.

64
00:03:39.843 --> 00:03:43.067
A, mortgage. B, pension. C, tax.

65
00:03:47.083 --> 00:03:49.153
The answer is A: mortgage.

66
00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.742
I wish I blank more time to study.

67
00:03:53.042 --> 00:03:55.906
A, have. B, had. C, will have.

68
00:03:59.917 --> 00:04:01.521
The answer is B: had.

69
00:04:03.017 --> 00:04:05.959
If only I blank that phone. It broke after a week!

70
00:04:06.259 --> 00:04:09.634
A, didn't buy. B, hadn't bought. C, don't buy.

71
00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.731
The answer is B: hadn't bought.

72
00:04:17.217 --> 00:04:19.832
When a company cannot pay its debts, it is blank.

73
00:04:20.132 --> 00:04:24.226
A, cost-effective. B, bankruptcy. C, stock market.

74
00:04:28.217 --> 00:04:30.472
The answer is B: bankruptcy.

75
00:04:31.983 --> 00:04:36.189
According to the text, many adults wish they had learned blank at school.

76
00:04:36.489 --> 00:04:40.154
A, financial literacy. B, history. C, sport.

77
00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:46.579
The answer is A: financial literacy.

78
00:04:48.083 --> 00:04:52.210
Now it's your turn to speak. What financial advice would you give your younger self?

79
00:04:57.510 --> 00:05:03.100
I wish I had started saving earlier. If only someone had explained how loans and interest work!

80
00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.086
Excellent answer, Nita!

81
00:05:06.083 --> 00:05:09.514
Great work today! You learned eight new words and wish and if only.

82
00:05:09.814 --> 00:05:13.430
Today's value is responsibility. Help at home and do your jobs.

83
00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:16.138
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

84
00:05:16.438 --> 00:05:19.137
Thank you, Mr. Daniel! Bye, everyone!

